From: SteamOS ARM alioth port
Subject: [PATCH] input: ft8756: retry the id read, and leave the ROM bootloader

With ft8756.poll=50 the point data register answers 161 polls in a row
with 62 bytes of 0xEF and a correct CRC, including while a finger is
held on the panel, and IRQ 152 stays at 0. The controller speaks the
protocol and reports no data, ever.

The driver puts it in that state itself. ft8756_check_chip_id() reads
register 0xA3 exactly once, 200ms after releasing reset, and on a
mismatch sends 0x55 0xAA -- the command that drops the chip into its
ROM bootloader. Nothing takes it out again: with ft8756.ignore_id=1
probe then continues, requests the interrupt and reports touches from
a chip that is sitting in ROM. Every register reads 0xEF and the INT
line never moves.

Two defects, both visible against the vendor driver:

  * The single read is unfair. FocalTech application firmware needs a
    few hundred milliseconds after reset, which is why the vendor
    driver polls 0xA3 (fts_wait_tp_to_valid) rather than reading it
    once. ft8756_wait_tp_valid() does the same, 20 tries 20ms apart,
    accepting any answer that is not the 0xEF/0xFF the chip gives
    while it has nothing valid to say -- the same two values
    ft8756_report() already treats that way in the point data.
    FT8756_MAX_RETRIES has been defined and unused since the driver
    was written.

  * Nothing leaves the ROM bootloader. When the id is unknown and
    ignore_id lets probe continue, reset the chip once more so it
    starts its application, and log what 0xA3 answers afterwards.
    That line is the measurement this round is for: if the
    application comes up, its value is this part's real chip id and
    the touch path can be judged on its own; if it times out again,
    the application firmware is not there and the next step is
    downloading it to PRAM.

This does not change the IRQ trigger type, which is still hardcoded
to IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_RISING against the DT's EDGE_FALLING. That question
is next and separate: ft8756.poll reads the point data without the
interrupt, so it can answer whether the chip reports at all before
the interrupt wiring is touched.

--- a/drivers/input/touchscreen/ft8756.c
+++ b/drivers/input/touchscreen/ft8756.c
@@ -27,6 +27,11 @@
 /* Misc */
 #define FT8756_NUM_SUPPLIES 3
 #define FT8756_MAX_RETRIES	3
+/* 20 x 20ms. The vendor driver's fts_wait_tp_to_valid() uses the same. */
+#define FT8756_ID_RETRIES	20
+/* What this controller answers while it has nothing valid to say. */
+#define FT8756_NO_DATA_1	0xEF
+#define FT8756_NO_DATA_2	0xFF
 
 #define DATA_CRC_EN 0x20
 #define WRITE_CMD   0x00
@@ -289,6 +294,34 @@
 	msleep(200);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Wait for the controller to answer out of its application firmware.
+ * 0xEF and 0xFF are what it returns while it has nothing valid to say --
+ * ft8756_report() reads both that way in the point data as well. The
+ * application takes a few hundred milliseconds to come up after reset,
+ * so a single read 200ms in can land before it; the vendor driver polls
+ * register 0xA3 for the same reason (fts_wait_tp_to_valid).
+ */
+static int ft8756_wait_tp_valid(struct ft8756_ts *ts, u8 *val)
+{
+	u8 v = FT8756_NO_DATA_1;
+	int i, ret;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < FT8756_ID_RETRIES; i++) {
+		ret = regmap_raw_read(ts->regmap, 0xA3, &v, 1);
+		if (!ret && v != FT8756_NO_DATA_1 && v != FT8756_NO_DATA_2) {
+			*val = v;
+			return 0;
+		}
+		msleep(20);
+	}
+
+	dev_warn(ts->dev, "no valid answer from 0xA3 in %d tries, last 0x%02x\n",
+		 FT8756_ID_RETRIES, v);
+	*val = v;
+	return -ETIMEDOUT;
+}
+
 static int ft8756_check_chip_id(struct ft8756_ts *ts)
 {
 	u16 chip_id = 0;
@@ -297,7 +330,10 @@
 
 	ft8756_reset(ts);
 
-	ret = regmap_raw_read(ts->regmap, 0xA3, &buf[0], 1);
+	ret = ft8756_wait_tp_valid(ts, &buf[0]);
+	if (ret)
+		goto fallback;
+
 	ret = regmap_raw_read(ts->regmap, 0x9F, &buf[1], 1);
 	chip_id = (buf[0] << 8) | buf[1];
 	if (chip_id != 0x5652 || ret) {
@@ -593,9 +629,26 @@
 skip_regulators:
 	ret = ft8756_check_chip_id(ts);
 	if (ret) {
+		u8 val;
+
 		if (!ft8756_ignore_id)
 			return ret;
 		dev_warn(dev, "unknown chip id, continuing (ft8756.ignore_id=1)\n");
+
+		/*
+		 * The id check's fallback path sends 0x55 0xAA, which puts
+		 * the controller into its ROM bootloader, and nothing took
+		 * it out again: every register then reads 0xEF and the INT
+		 * line never moves, which is exactly what this phone shows.
+		 * Reset once more so the chip runs its application before
+		 * we start asking it for touches, and log what it answers
+		 * -- that one line says whether the application comes up
+		 * at all.
+		 */
+		ft8756_reset(ts);
+		if (!ft8756_wait_tp_valid(ts, &val))
+			dev_info(dev, "0xA3 reads 0x%02x after a second reset\n",
+				 val);
 	}
 
 	ret = ft8756_input_dev_config(ts);
